    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — coverage is complete.\n\n**Summary: 17 PASS / 2 FAIL**\n\nThe core to-do functionality is solid: task CRUD, priority/deadline sorting (including no-deadline tasks sorting last), active/completed views with accurate counts, non-destructive completion, empty-title guards, blocked-task warnings, and both dependency tooltips (warning badge and dashed SVG connectors) all behaved as specified. Cycle prevention was notably robust — cycle-creating tasks are omitted from the prerequisite picker for both direct and 2-level indirect dependencies.\n\nTwo defects were found:\n\n- **FT-3 (FAIL)** — Blocked tasks are completely uneditable. The Edit button carries the HTML `disabled` attribute, and no alternative edit path exists (clicking the card opens no dialog). The requirement is that a blocked task's title and deadline lock while **description and priority remain editable**; here all four fields are locked.\n\n- **CS-22 (FAIL)** — Invalid state reachable. After completing a prerequisite, completing its dependent, then reopening the prerequisite, the dependent task remains in the Completed view *while* displaying `Prerequisite \"Buy groceries\" incomplete`. The reopen proceeds silently, so the task ends up simultaneously completed and blocked — the system neither blocks the reopen nor cascades the dependent back to incomplete.\n\nBoth failures cluster around the dependency-state machine: FT-3 is an over-restriction on blocked tasks, while CS-22 is an under-restriction on un-completing prerequisites."
  }
